 {
  "coord": "1:26",
  "verdict": "FAIL",
  "category": "hedge",
  "note": "\"generally compelled\" strengthened to \"almost always forced\".",
  "replacement": "And when she had gone, Socrates sat up on the couch, bent his leg and rubbed it, and said as he rubbed: How strange a thing is what people call pleasure, and how curiously it is related to pain, which might be thought its opposite. The two are never present in a man at the same moment, and yet anyone who pursues one is usually forced to take the other; they are two bodies joined by a single head. And I cannot help thinking that, if Aesop had thought of them, he would have made a fable about God trying to end their quarrel and, when he could not, fastening their heads together. That is why, when one comes, the other follows. I know it from my own experience now: the pain in my leg, caused by the chain, seems to be followed by pleasure."
 },
